Z||000000||firstfiero||03-22-2001||01:53 PM||||    This is a dumb question but under the hood it tells me to read owners manual befor adding coolant. Theirs nothing I should know special to a fiero is their? Or is it the same old careful when and wear safty goggles type crap thats in all owners manual.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||01:56 PM||||Because the radiator is so far removed from the engine, getting air pockets can be a problem when refilling the coolant system. <P>Check some of the threads coolant refills!||||reg||6
Z||000002||Oreif||03-22-2001||02:08 PM||||Also the radiator is a lot lower than the engine. So if you remove the front radiator cap coolant will flow out quickly. Any air pockets will dampen the cooling system. Hence you would get a lot of over-heating or drastic temperature swings.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mr.Frosty||03-22-2001||02:09 PM||||Yes,the filling procedure on the fiero is special indeed.I had trouble years back with airpockets gettin in the system and causing temp. spikes.I fixed it by burping the system while running(messy).It's no a bad idea to change those old coolant caps front and rear.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||8
Z||000004||Alex4mula||03-22-2001||02:50 PM||||I don't know which types of Fieros do you have but I would be scare with you comments to touch anything there. I can open my front cap and maybe less than 1oz of coolant may flow out. If the system was properly filled you can open the rad cap to add coolant there or in the overflow container without any problem. Just make sure the car is parked in a flat (level) surface.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||03-22-2001||03:09 PM||||Fiero's should be filled from the back cap only. It is the highest point.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||firstfiero||03-22-2001||03:15 PM||||what do you mean by back cap...the one at the engine?||||reg||5
Z||000007||firstfiero||03-22-2001||03:20 PM||||   by the way I did have temp. spikes. My car usually stays pretty far below the half. I was sitting ideling while my wife was putting gas in her car when I noticed the temp. guage slowly going up until it hit a little below the red. I shut of the engine  and when I started her back up she was still above the half way mark. As soon as I pulled out the guage dropped like a brick down it quarter where it usually stays. I never saw it do that befor.....thanks for the help||||reg||9
Z||000008||fropuff||03-22-2001||04:17 PM||||Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR>   By the way I did have temp. spikes. My car usually stays pretty far below the half. I was sitting ideling while my wife was putting gas in her car when I noticed the temp. guage slowly going up until it hit a little below the red. I shut of the engine  and when I started her back up she was still above the half way mark. As soon as I pulled out the guage dropped like a brick down it quarter where it usually stays. I never saw it do that befor.....thanks for the help.<P>That's what happens when you have an air pocket, or when there's a leak in the system.  If there's a leak, it probably won't go away until fixed.  I had a loose clamp on a radiator tube, and it made it almost overheat.  Needed to tow it home.  I also had a few air pockets.  After I had refilled the system, the gauge went up to around 205.  Went back down and now it's fine.<P>||||reg||1
